[Music]  with one spin-off underway and another  in the queue yellowstone is showing no  signs of slowing down when it comes to  keeping viewers coming back or tuning in  for the first time week after week  without a doubt a major component of the  serious appeal is its most rugged  anti-hero rip wheeler whose devotion to  john dutton and yellowstone ranch  appears to know no bounds and yet there  are bounds when it comes to what  audiences are willing to accept with  regard to the often criminal inevitably  violent actions rip feels he must take  in order to defend the duttons and their  ranch throughout season 4 another  beloved character lloyd pierce has  butted heads with one of the other ranch  hands walker walker stole lloyd's  girlfriend and lloyd has been unable to  keep his jealousy and anger at bay ever  since after lloyd breaks the no fighting  in the bunkhouse rule in episode 4 rip  and john ultimately decide the two  should work their issues out by pounding  the crap out of one another though jon  has one more wrinkle to add  i want you to make an example out of  last man standing  rip not only pummels lloyd repeatedly in  episode 4 after the latter's initial  fight with walker but he also beats him  down aggressively in episode 6 and  graphically smashes lloyd's hand with  his boot lloyd's second beating  importantly isn't rip's way of  reiterating this  no fighting on this ranch you want to  fight somebody you come fight me i'll  fight you all day  when rip destroys his best friend's hand  it's because lloyd refuses to back down  from a fight that john dutton ordered  rip to have lloyd and walker undertake  until they could work out their issues  by the time rip nobly shoulders the  burden of taking lloyd down for good  lloyd's been continually fist fighting  with walker for well over an hour prior  to the premiere of season 4 fans were  already speculating about rip and  lloyd's fight from the trailers and had  some pretty distinct opinions about what  they felt it would take to justify such  a thing as one fan wrote on the series  subreddit lloyd would have to commit a  cardinal sin for rip to beat him up  lloyd's cardinal sin on the surface was  stabbing walker scratch below that  surface however and it becomes clear  that his real sin was putting his own  personal grudge against walker over the  safety of the ranch or so we're told  however it's a bit more complicated than  that as viewers will recall casey was  supposed to kill walker in season 2  after the singer saw rip help jaime  cover up a murder casey however couldn't  bring himself to killwalker and misstep  rip and lloyd discover in season 3  episode 9 but walker doesn't actually  return to the yellowstone ranch on his  own walker shows up in dillon had lloyd  and rip simply walked away from the bar  and pretended they never saw walker they  wouldn't be obligated to keep him under  the thumb of the ranch at all since he  clearly wasn't interested in reporting  anything he saw to the authorities  thus the better part of the situation  that leads to rip being forced to beat  down on lloyd is a situation largely of  his own making and subsequently  difficult to chalk up to just another  instance of cowboy justice add to that  the fact that walker and lloyd's entire  conflict does little if anything to  further the plot of the series and you  have the makings of a scene that simply  crosses one bridge to many when it comes  to anti-heroes like rip audiences can  accept otherwise unacceptable levels of  violence since they're tethered to the  narrative these acts would be appalling  to our sense of right and wrong in the  real world but within the fictitious  universe they can be justifiable it's  only when that violence spills outside  the bounds of that justification either  because we've gained too much sympathy  for the individual on the receiving end  or because it doesn't come off as a  legitimately necessary action that our  willful suspension of real-world  morality begins to break down  viewers not only have enormous amounts  of sympathy for lloyd but it's also  difficult to understand the thinking  that went into throwing two ranch hands  and integral employees into a  gladiator-style fight to the finish only  to then punish one of those individuals  for doing exactly what he was told it's  so difficult to understand in fact that  several viewers on one reddit red  theorize that the scene only really  existed because the show is setting up a  situation for rip to take lloyd for  granted and then realize he was wrong  whether that theory pans out or not  we'll just have to wait and see check  out one of our newest videos right here  plus even more looper videos about  yellowstone are coming soon subscribe to  our youtube channel and hit the bell so  you don't miss a single one  you

